00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:02.510
Way back in the late 1980s,
almost 30 years ago, Goldreich,

00:06:02.511 --> 00:06:05.730
Micali and
Wigderson gave a protocol for

00:06:05.731 --> 00:06:08.080
securely computing
any function F.

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:14.610
However, the round
complexity of this protocol

00:06:14.611 --> 00:06:19.630
grows with the depth of
the circuit representation.

00:06:19.631 --> 00:06:22.800
So let me first explain to
you what I mean by the round

00:06:22.801 --> 00:06:23.380
complexity.

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:28.220
So the round complexity is the
number of sequential messages

00:06:28.221 --> 00:06:30.480
exchanged between the parties.

00:06:30.481 --> 00:06:33.380
So if I have three parties.

00:06:33.381 --> 00:06:36.330
And let's say the first
party sends a message to

00:06:36.331 --> 00:06:38.000
the second party.

00:06:38.001 --> 00:06:39.060
Depending on this message,

00:06:39.061 --> 00:06:42.790
the second party sends another
message to the third party.

00:06:42.791 --> 00:06:45.480
And the third party can
again send a message to

00:06:45.481 --> 00:06:47.020
the first party.

00:06:47.021 --> 00:06:49.390
So the number of rounds of this
protocol is actually three.

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:54.020
Because the number of
sequential messages is three.

00:06:54.021 --> 00:06:56.930
The second party cannot send
a message to the third party

00:06:56.931 --> 00:06:59.070
unless it receives the message
from the first party.

00:07:01.390 --> 00:07:03.770
Okay, so now we know what
is the number of rounds.

00:07:03.771 --> 00:07:06.620
And let me explain to you

00:07:06.621 --> 00:07:09.610
what I mean by the depth of
this acute representation.

00:07:09.611 --> 00:07:13.990
So we called the function
that we want to compute "as

00:07:13.991 --> 00:07:15.630
if", right?

00:07:15.631 --> 00:07:18.790
The drawing function that has
to be computed on the private

00:07:18.791 --> 00:07:20.450
inputs of the pattern.

00:07:20.451 --> 00:07:25.495
If you write this function "if"
as a bullion circuit using and,

00:07:25.496 --> 00:07:30.356
or, and not Git, then the this
depth of this bullion circuit is

00:07:30.357 --> 00:07:34.700
what I mean by the depth of
the circuit representation.

00:07:34.701 --> 00:07:37.456
So the number of rounds of
the Goldreich, Micali and

00:07:37.457 --> 00:07:40.586
Wigderson Protocol which we
will call as the GMW protocol,

00:07:40.587 --> 00:07:43.416
grows with the depth of
the circuit representation.

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:49.070
So why do we care about
the number of rounds, or

00:07:49.071 --> 00:07:51.250
in other words why is the number
of rounds important for

00:07:51.251 --> 00:07:51.950
applications?

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:57.370
For high latency applications
the number of rounds is

00:07:57.371 --> 00:08:00.380
the major factor in determining
the efficiency of the protocol.

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:05.529
So let's say that even in the
previous scenario we consider

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:09.261
there is a hospital in Seattle
and there is another hospital in

00:08:09.262 --> 00:08:12.991
New York and they want to send a
message from Seattle to New York

00:08:12.992 --> 00:08:15.833
and the message back
from New York to Seattle.

00:08:15.834 --> 00:08:18.853
So it take a few hundred
milliseconds for this message to

00:08:18.854 --> 00:08:21.553
travel to New York and
another few hundred message

00:08:21.554 --> 00:08:25.043
A few hundred milliseconds for
another message to travel back.

00:08:25.044 --> 00:08:28.785
So the meantime,
this party cannot do anything,

00:08:28.786 --> 00:08:32.110
soi t has to just
keep on waiting.

00:08:32.111 --> 00:08:34.600
And if the number of rounds
is in fact of the order of

00:08:34.601 --> 00:08:38.650
thousands then a lot of time
is wasted by the parties in

00:08:38.651 --> 00:08:40.900
just waiting for the messages
to go back and forth.

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:46.460
And for complicated functions,
such as the ESQ,

00:08:46.461 --> 00:08:50.760
the depth of the circuit could
be in the order of thousands.

00:08:50.761 --> 00:08:54.870
So the GMW protocol will take
order of thousand rounds to

00:08:54.871 --> 00:08:58.037
compute even a AES circuit.

00:08:58.038 --> 00:09:01.660
And unlike
the computational power,

00:09:01.661 --> 00:09:05.248
which is growing according
to the [INAUDIBLE].

00:09:05.249 --> 00:09:08.920
And the bandwidth of
the network, which is growing,

00:09:08.921 --> 00:09:12.710
which is improving almost every
day by using better materials.

00:09:12.711 --> 00:09:15.730
The latency of the network
is somewhat fixed, and

00:09:15.731 --> 00:09:17.400
cannot be improved
beyond a certain point.

00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:22.440
This is because the latency is
determined by the geographical

00:09:22.441 --> 00:09:24.840
distance between the parties and
the speed of light.

00:09:24.841 --> 00:09:26.080
Both of which
cannot be improved.

00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:31.550
So the takeaway message from
this slide is that for high

00:09:31.551 --> 00:09:36.220
latency applications the number
of rounds is very important.

00:09:36.221 --> 00:09:39.060
And we need to optimize
the number of rounds in order to

00:09:39.061 --> 00:09:43.060
have a practical multi-party
computation protocol.

00:09:44.479 --> 00:09:45.112
Okay?

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:49.310
So an actual question would be
to what is the minimum number

00:09:49.311 --> 00:09:51.050
