garbled circuits.

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:34.581
So garbled circuit is a
foundational concept introduced

00:12:34.582 --> 00:12:36.470
in the seminal paper of Yao.

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:40.920
And it's one of the most
important tools in

00:12:40.921 --> 00:12:43.550
any cryptographer's
tool kit I would say.

00:12:43.551 --> 00:12:47.150
And it has found applications
in almost all areas of

00:12:47.151 --> 00:12:48.310
cryptographing.

00:12:48.311 --> 00:12:50.800
So let's see what we mean
by a garbled circuit.

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:56.470
So we have a function f that
takes n bits of input and

00:12:56.471 --> 00:12:57.410
outputs n bits.

00:12:58.890 --> 00:13:04.270
Garbled circuits allow you to
encode this function f into

00:13:04.271 --> 00:13:09.900
a garbled circuit f tilde along
with a pair of labels for

00:13:09.901 --> 00:13:11.650
each input y of this function f.

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:16.270
So since f has n bits of input

00:13:16.271 --> 00:13:21.070
this garbled circuit we
have n types of labels.

00:13:21.071 --> 00:13:25.330
And each pair has one label
corresponding to the bit 0,

00:13:25.331 --> 00:13:31.260
and another pair which
corresponds to the bit 1.

00:13:31.261 --> 00:13:36.946
Okay, So this garbled
circuit f tilde along

00:13:36.947 --> 00:13:42.080
with the labels corresponding
to the bits of an input x.

00:13:42.081 --> 00:13:46.020
Allow one to evaluate and
compute f of x.

00:13:46.021 --> 00:13:49.580
So let's first consider a plain
boolean circuit without

00:13:49.581 --> 00:13:50.630
any garbling.

00:13:50.631 --> 00:13:55.630
So let's say it has n input bias
and giving either zeroes or

00:13:55.631 --> 00:13:58.810
ones to each of those input
biases allows you evaluate

00:13:58.811 --> 00:14:00.170
the function and get its output.

00:14:01.460 --> 00:14:04.560
In garbled circuit is somewhat
very similar to a Boolean

00:14:04.561 --> 00:14:06.370
circuit in this respect.

00:14:06.371 --> 00:14:08.470
So the garbled circuit f tilde,

00:14:08.471 --> 00:14:12.510
instead of choosing the actual
values of the Inputs, you choose

00:14:12.511 --> 00:14:14.670
the labels corresponding
to the bits of the input.

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:18.410
If your first bit of
the input is zero,

00:14:18.411 --> 00:14:21.320
then you choose the label
corresponding to zero.

00:14:21.321 --> 00:14:23.530
Let's say the last bit
of your input is one,

00:14:23.531 --> 00:14:25.330
then you choose the label
corresponding to one.

00:14:26.850 --> 00:14:30.280
So if you choose these
labels from zero or one,

00:14:30.281 --> 00:14:33.340
according to the bits of your
input, then there is a special

00:14:33.341 --> 00:14:38.089
evaluation procedure that
allows you to obtain f (x).

00:14:41.632 --> 00:14:45.046
Are there any
questions about this?

00:14:45.047 --> 00:14:50.352
Okay, but the special property
of the garbled circuit

00:14:50.353 --> 00:14:56.130
is that this f data along with
these labels leaks only f of x.

00:14:57.680 --> 00:15:00.510
So this is not proper of
plain circuit because

00:15:00.511 --> 00:15:04.420
if you are give a plain
circuit and bits of the input

00:15:04.421 --> 00:15:07.220
then it eats everything about
the circuit and the input.

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:12.485
But the garbled circuit is
special in this aspect,

00:15:12.486 --> 00:15:15.100
right, the garbled
circuit f tilde

00:15:15.101 --> 00:15:18.090
along with these labels
leaks only f of x.

00:15:18.091 --> 00:15:21.310
So you can treat this garbled
circuit along with these label

00:15:21.311 --> 00:15:27.020
as a black box that hides
everything about f and x.

00:15:27.021 --> 00:15:31.480
Apart from just f of x, and
importantly, this is true

00:15:31.481 --> 00:15:35.040
only if I have a single
label per each input wire.

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:41.600
Even for a one-input, well, if I
get access to both the labels,

00:15:41.601 --> 00:15:43.091
then all security bits are off.

00:15:45.899 --> 00:15:49.238
